    <body>
      <sec id="article-30895.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>Uterine inversion is a rare but life-threatening obstetric emergency characterized by descent of the uterine fundus into or beyond the uterine cavity, most commonly during the third stage of labor. Frequently associated with severe postpartum hemorrhage and hemodynamic instability, this condition requires rapid recognition and immediate intervention to prevent shock, organ injury, or death. This course details the pathophysiology of uterine inversion, often involving puerperal mechanisms and more diagnostically challenging nonpuerperal cases linked to uterine tumors. Participants will gain an in-depth understanding of the risk factors, clinical presentations, and evidence-based management strategies for uterine inversion, including prompt manual reduction, hemodynamic stabilization, and escalation to pharmacologic or surgical interventions when necessary. This activity for healthcare professionals is designed to enhance learners' competence in identifying uterine inversion, appropriately using imaging, and implementing an effective interprofessional approach when managing this condition, thereby optimizing maternal outcomes and reducing morbidity and mortality.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Determine&#x000a0;the possible etiologies of uterine inversion.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Identify the typical presentation of a patient with uterine inversion to enable rapid recognition.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Implement immediate evidence-based management steps for&#x000a0;uterine inversion to reduce morbidity.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Coordinate interprofessional team responses to optimize care and outcomes for patients with uterine inversion.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=30895&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=30895">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-30895.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>Uterine inversion refers to the descent of the uterine fundus into the uterine cavity, through the cervical os, or beyond the vaginal introitus. Classification includes puerperal and nonpuerperal forms, depending on the clinical context. Approximately 85% of cases occur during the postpartum period.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30895.r1">[1]</xref> Puerperal uterine inversion represents a rare but life-threatening obstetric emergency, typically arising during the third stage of labor with placental delivery and strongly associated with postpartum hemorrhage, defined as blood loss &#x02265;1000 mL or blood loss accompanied by signs or symptoms of hypovolemia.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30895.r2">[2]</xref> Blood loss frequently requires transfusion and may progress to hypovolemic shock. Higher incidence has been reported following cesarean delivery.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30895.r3">[3]</xref></p>
        <p>Prompt recognition allows immediate attempts at manual replacement of the inverted fundus. Failure of manual reduction warrants consideration of uterine relaxants and surgical intervention. Rapid, coordinated action by an interprofessional team remains essential. In nonobstetrical settings, uterine inversion has been reported in association with uterine tumors. Diagnosis in nonpuerperal cases remains challenging due to clinical resemblance to vaginal or cervical tumors and potential misidentification as pelvic organ prolapse.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-30895.s3" sec-type="Etiology">
        <title>Etiology</title>
        <p>The exact etiology of uterine inversion is unknown. The most commonly proposed causes are excessive umbilical cord traction and fundal pressure during the third stage of labor, especially with a fundal placental attachment. Other possible risk factors for uterine inversion include rapid labor, invasive placentation, manual removal of placenta, short umbilical cord, use of uterine-relaxing agents, uterine overdistension, fetal macrosomia, nulliparity, placenta previa, connective tissue disorders (Marfan syndrome and Ehlers-Danlos syndrome), and a history of uterine inversion in a previous pregnancy. However, in most cases, no risk factors are identified, making this condition unpredictable.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30895.r4">[4]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30895.r5">[5]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30895.r6">[6]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Uterine inversion is broadly described as incomplete or complete, depending on whether the fundus remains within the uterine cavity or extends beyond the cervix, with more severe cases progressing to prolapse outside the vaginal introitus. This spectrum is more precisely characterized using a 4-stage classification system (stages I to IV) based on the degree of inversion.</p>
        <p>
<bold>The 4 Stages of Uterine Inversion</bold>
</p>
        <p>Uterine inversion is classified into the following categories based on the extent of fundal descent:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>First-degree</bold>: The uterine fundus is depressed but remains within the uterine cavity.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Second-degree:</bold>&#x000a0;The fundus passes through the cervix but does not extend to the introitus.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Third-degree</bold>: The fundus protrudes to or beyond the vaginal opening.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Fourth-degree</bold>: Both the uterus and vagina protrude outside the perineum.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30895.r7">[7]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30895.r8">[8]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30895.r7">[7]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Classifications of Uterine Inversion</bold>
</p>
        <p>Uterine inversion is also divided based on&#x000a0;timing after delivery into the following classifications:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Acute</bold>: occurs 24 hours or less&#x000a0;postpartum (most common)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Subacute</bold>: occurs between 24 hours and&#x000a0;4 weeks postpartum</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Chronic</bold>: occurs longer than 1 month postpartum&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30895.r9">[9]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30895.r10">[10]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>This temporal classification is useful because the presentation, management, and likelihood of successful nonsurgical reduction can vary with how long the inversion has been present.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Common Risk Factors</bold>
</p>
        <p>Identifying factors that place patients at high risk for uterine inversion can help in the timely recognition of this condition. Common risk factors include:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Fundal placental implantation&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Excessive traction on the umbilical cord before placental separation&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Uterine atony&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Morbidly adherent placenta (placenta accreta spectrum)&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Short umbilical cord&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Excessive fundal pressure applied by the delivery attendant</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Weakness of the uterine wall due to prior surgery, tumors, or other pathology&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-30895.s4" sec-type="Epidemiology">
        <title>Epidemiology</title>
        <p>Uterine inversion is a rare obstetric complication, occurring in approximately 1 in 3,400 deliveries<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30895.r11">[11]</xref>, with reported incidence ranging from 1 in 2,000 to 1 in 20,000 vaginal births. This condition is&#x000a0;somewhat more frequent during cesarean deliveries, with an incidence of 1 in 1,860. In a 24-year retrospective study, the incidence of uterine inversion&#x000a0;after cesarean&#x000a0;delivery&#x000a0;was 1 in 1,860 deliveries compared to 1 in 3,737 following vaginal&#x000a0;deliveries.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30895.r3">[3]</xref></p>
        <p>Despite its rarity, the condition demands rapid identification and intervention due to the potential for rapid clinical deterioration and even death from hemorrhage.&#x000a0;About 38% of cases of uterine inversion are associated with postpartum hemorrhage, 22% necessitate blood transfusion, and roughly 6% require surgical intervention.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30895.r11">[11]</xref> Uterine inversion has decreased 4-fold after the introduction of active management during the third stage of labor.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30895.r11">[11]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30895.r3">[3]</xref>&#x000a0;Only a small number of cases have been reported over the past decade, likely due to improved prenatal care and a higher rate of deliveries in healthcare facilities.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30895.r12">[12]</xref></p>
        <p>At least&#x000a0;1 uterine inversion has been reported after an incomplete medically induced abortion at 14 weeks, as well as a postmenopausal woman with a benign leiomyoma.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30895.r13">[13]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30895.r14">[14]</xref>&#x000a0;The youngest reported uterine inversion occurred in a 9-year-old, which was a nonpuerperal inversion from a large M&#x000fc;llerian adenosarcoma of the uterus.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30895.r15">[15]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30895.r16">[16]</xref>&#x000a0;Nonpuerperal uterine inversions,&#x000a0;eg, this, are even more rare, with only about 150 cases reported between 1887 and 2006.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30895.r17">[17]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-30895.s5" sec-type="Pathophysiology">
        <title>Pathophysiology</title>
        <p>Although the precise cause is unclear, uterine inversion is most often considered iatrogenic. Proposed mechanisms include excessive traction on the umbilical cord and application of fundal pressure before the placenta has separated, particularly in the setting of uterine atony or a fundally implanted placenta.&#x000a0;However, a causal relationship between fundal pressure and cord traction and uterine inversion has not been proven, as these are common maneuvers performed during the third stage of labor, while uterine inversion remains rare. Uterine inversion is strongly associated with placental abnormalities, with prolonged labor being another important risk factor. Additional contributing factors include uterine atony in the immediate postpartum period, a dilated cervix, abnormal placental adherence, a short umbilical cord, and rapid uterine decompression.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30895.r7">[7]</xref></p>
        <p>Uterine inversion frequently causes significant postpartum hemorrhage because the inwardly turned fundus prevents effective uterine contraction. On average, blood loss with acute uterine inversion&#x000a0;has been estimated to be 1,775 mL.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30895.r18">[18]</xref> The bleeding can be worsened by disruption at the placental attachment site, as the inverted endometrium is stretched.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30895.r12">[12]</xref>&#x000a0;Similarly, nonpuerperal uterine inversions are most commonly associated with tumors that exert a traction force on the uterine fundus, with&#x000a0;70% to 85% being benign submucosal leiomyomas, leaving 15% to 30% of cases associated with malignant tumours, particularly sarcomas.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30895.r1">[1]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-30895.s6" sec-type="Histopathology">
        <title>Histopathology</title>
        <p>In nonpuerperal uterine inversion, histopathology is important for determining the cause. Nonpuerperal uterine inversions may be due to myomas, polyps, or other uterine tumors, such as leiomyosarcomas, rhabdomyosarcomas, mixed Mullerian tumors, or cervical and endometrial carcinomas.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30895.r19">[19]</xref>&#x000a0;Malignancies should always be considered in cases of nonpuerperal uterine inversion.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30895.r20">[20]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-30895.s7" sec-type="History and Physical">
        <title>History and Physical</title>
        <p>Signs of uterine inversion include severe abdominal pain, a lump in the vagina, a palpable mass&#x000a0;inside or at the&#x000a0;vaginal introitus, abdominal tenderness, the absence of a uterine fundus on abdominal palpation, and severe postpartum hemorrhage.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30895.r12">[12]</xref>&#x000a0;Uterine inversion frequently presents&#x000a0;with very brisk vaginal bleeding, pelvic or abdominal pain, and signs of hemodynamic instability, including hemorrhagic shock. On examination, a fundal mass may be palpated within the vagina, while abdominal palpation may reveal an absent or &#x0201c;cupped&#x0201d; fundus. If the clinical diagnosis is unclear, a bedside ultrasound should be performed promptly.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30895.r7">[7]</xref></p>
        <p>Uterine inversion is a clinical diagnosis and should be suspected when the fundus is not palpable abdominally. The sudden onset of very brisk vaginal bleeding during uterine inversion can lead to hemodynamic instability in the mother. Traditionally, the shock has been considered disproportionate to blood loss. This&#x000a0;may be explained by parasympathetic stimulation resulting from tissue stretching while inverted. However, the need for blood transfusion should be carefully evaluated, as blood loss is frequently greatly underestimated.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30895.r9">[9]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30895.r21">[21]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-30895.s8" sec-type="Evaluation">
        <title>Evaluation</title>
        <p>
<bold>Ultrasound Imaging</bold>
</p>
        <p>The diagnosis of uterine inversion is primarily made clinically with a bimanual examination, during which the uterine fundus is palpated in the lower uterine segment or within the vagina.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30895.r22">[22]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30895.r9">[9]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30895.r23">[23]</xref>&#x000a0;If ultrasound is used, characteristic&#x000a0;findings can aid in the diagnosis. A partial or complete inversion will result in poor visualization of the fluid content expected during the third stage of labor, or in a Y-shaped configuration caused by the invaginated fundus, further displacing the&#x000a0;2 opposing uterine walls.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>In the sagittal view,&#x000a0;an inward collapse of the uterine fundus may be seen. With complete inversion, the uterine deformation&#x000a0;forms a "U" shape.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30895.r1">[1]</xref>&#x000a0;On transverse imaging, a relatively echogenic mass compared with the surrounding hypoechoic myometrium is seen, creating a characteristic &#x0201c;target&#x0201d; or "bull's-eye".<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30895.r24">[24]</xref>&#x000a0;The target sign has been further described as the hyperechoic fundus of the uterus, surrounded by fluid within the space between the cervical or vaginal wall and the inverted fundus.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30895.r1">[1]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Magnetic Resonance Imaging</bold>
</p>
        <p>For chronic presentations, magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) can help confirm the diagnosis, with T2-weighted imaging best demonstrating the distorted uterine anatomy and medially displaced adnexal structures.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30895.r7">[7]</xref>&#x000a0;Outside of pregnancy,&#x000a0;MRI is recommended in cases with an unusual vaginal mass to evaluate possible uterine inversion secondary to uterine pathology.&#x000a0;MRI can confirm uterine inversion, define its type, and identify any underlying mass. Typical findings include a U-shaped uterine cavity on sagittal and coronal views due to fundal invagination with loss of the normal fundal contour, and a &#x0201c;bull&#x02019;s-eye&#x0201d; appearance on axial images. Visualization of centrally displaced round ligaments further supports the diagnosis. MRI also provides a detailed assessment of the causative lesion and surrounding structures.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30895.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30895.r25">[25]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-30895.s9" sec-type="Treatment / Management">
        <title>Treatment / Management</title>
        <p>Prompt recognition, immediate action, maintaining hemodynamic stability, and timely reduction are the keys to successful treatment.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30895.r7">[7]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30895.r9">[9]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Initial Management</bold>
</p>
        <p>Initiating immediate intervention is critical in the management of uterine inversion. These steps include:&#x000a0;</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Call for assistance (obstetrics, anesthesia, and nursing).</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Request immediate availability of blood products.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Establish&#x000a0;2 large-bore IVs and initiate rapid infusion of crystalloids.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Discontinue any&#x000a0;uterotonics (eg, oxytocin)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Do not remove the placenta if still attached, as this can exacerbate hemorrhage.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Manual </bold>
<bold>Repositioning</bold>
</p>
        <p>Clinicians should attempt immediate uterine replacement by applying steady pressure with the palm or closed fist on the inverted fundus, directing it along the vaginal axis toward the umbilicus.&#x000a0;Manual reduction begins by placing the inverted uterine fundus and body in the palm of the dominant hand. Steady, continuous pressure is then applied in an upward direction, following the axis of the pelvis rather than directly vertical. The goal is to gently guide the inverted portion back through the cervical ring, with the segment closest to the cervix being reduced first. Simultaneously, the nondominant hand may be placed on the abdomen to provide counterpressure and help direct the uterus during repositioning.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30895.r7">[7]</xref></p>
        <p>Once the uterus has been restored to its normal anatomic position, the operator maintains a hand at the fundus, often as a closed fist, to support uterine tone while administering uterotonic agents. As effective, coordinated uterine contractions are established, the hand is gradually withdrawn while ensuring the uterus remains well contracted and in proper position.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30895.r2">[2]</xref></p>
        <p>The O&#x02019;Sullivan hydrostatic method uses fluid pressure to reposition an inverted uterus and is a useful option when manual reduction is unsuccessful, with reported effectiveness in both acute and subacute cases. The technique involves placing the patient in the Trendelenburg position, creating a seal at the vaginal introitus with the clinician&#x02019;s hand, and infusing warm saline or sterile water into the vagina, often in large volumes, to generate steady pressure. This pressure gradually pushes the uterine fundus back through the cervix into its normal position, with progress assessed clinically or by ultrasound. This approach is noninvasive, may not require general anesthesia, and can be effective when used alone or in combination with manual techniques. Following successful reduction, uterotonics should be administered promptly to maintain uterine tone and prevent recurrence.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30895.r26">[26]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Tocolytics</bold>
</p>
        <p>If initial uterine replacement is not successful, then tocolytics may be used.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30895.r2">[2]</xref>&#x000a0;Commonly utilized agents include:&#x000a0;</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Terbutaline:</bold>&#x000a0;A dosage of 250&#x000a0;&#x000b5;g&#x000a0;intravenously&#x000a0;(IV) is recommended.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Nitroglycerin</bold>: A dosage of 50&#x02013;100&#x000a0;&#x000b5;g&#x000a0;IV is preferred by many due to its&#x000a0;rapid onset of uterine relaxation. Incremental dosing may be used.&#x000a0;Reported effective doses range from 50 mcg to 500 &#x000b5;g IV as an initial bolus, with up to&#x000a0;3 repeated injections of 50 to 250 &#x000b5;g IV if needed.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30895.r27">[27]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Sublingual nitroglycerin</bold>: A tablet or metered-dose spray is an alternative to IV dosing.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30895.r28">[28]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Magnesium sulfate</bold>: A dosage of&#x000a0;4 g IV is recommended.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Halothane</bold>: This agent is a<bold>&#x000a0;</bold>general anesthetic with uterine relaxant properties.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Nitroglycerin may cause hypotension and headache. A drop in systolic blood pressure to around 93 mm Hg can occur within approximately 2 minutes of administration and is typically responsive to small doses of ephedrine.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30895.r29">[29]</xref>&#x000a0;If manual replacement is unsuccessful with the placenta in situ, consider removing the placenta and reattempting repositioning. However, waiting until uterotonics and blood are at the bedside before removing the placenta is recommended, as hemorrhage is likely to worsen after removal.</p>
        <p>Once the uterus is repositioned, the following steps should be performed:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Discontinue tocolytics.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Begin uterotonics (eg, oxytocin infusion) to maintain uterine tone.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30895.r2">[2]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Apply bimanual compression or leave 2 fingers at the fundus until uterine contraction is sustained&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Monitor closely for reinversion; in select cases, intrauterine balloon tamponade (eg, Bakri balloon) may be used to maintain uterine position&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Surgical Management</bold>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>If manual attempts fail, operative management is required. A common reason for failure is a constriction ring at the cervico-uterine junction. Surgical interventions include:&#x000a0;</p>
        <list list-type="order">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Anesthesia</bold>: A rapid-acting halogenated inhalational agent is administered to promote uterine relaxation.&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Laparotomy</bold>: Repositioning of the uterus is reattempted via a combined vaginal-abdominal approach.&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Huntington Procedure</bold>: Atraumatic (Allis) clamps are placed on the cornua near the round ligaments, and gentle traction is applied upward through an abdominal incision.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30895.r2">[2]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Haultain Procedure</bold>: If the Huntington method fails, perform a posterior vertical incision through the constricting cervical ring to allow repositioning with the Huntington method.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30895.r2">[2]</xref> Once successful, repair the uterine incision.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Antonelli&#x02019;s Method:</bold>&#x000a0;A Silastic vacuum cup is positioned on the uterine fundus from above through an abdominal incision, and negative pressure is applied to help reposition the uterus to its normal anatomy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30895.r2">[2]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Laparoscopically</bold>
<bold>guided approach</bold>: To achieve uterine visualization and reduction a&#x000a0;minimally invasive approach, especially in nonpuerperal uterine inversion, may be a feasible option.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30895.r20">[20]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30895.r12">[12]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30895.r20">[20]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>After surgical uterine repositioning, the following steps should be performed:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Administer uterotonics to sustain contractions</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Monitor for reinversion&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Consider compression sutures in recurrent cases&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-30895.s10" sec-type="Differential Diagnosis">
        <title>Differential Diagnosis</title>
        <p>The conditions that cause a lump in the vagina and lead to postpartum collapse need to be excluded, including:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Severe atony of the uterus</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Uterovaginal prolapse</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Fibroid (prolapsed)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Polyp</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Retained placental fragments</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Placenta accreta spectrum&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Uterine rupture with severe postpartum hemorrhage</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Large blood clots in the uterus or vagina</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Coagulopathy</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-30895.s11" sec-type="Prognosis">
        <title>Prognosis</title>
        <p>The prognosis of uterine inversion is generally favorable with prompt recognition and immediate management, but outcomes can deteriorate rapidly if diagnosis is delayed. The condition is associated with significant morbidity, primarily due to postpartum hemorrhage in 65% of cases, with over 47% of patients requiring blood transfusions.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30895.r12">[12]</xref> Hemorrhagic shock may follow and is sometimes disproportionate to the degree of blood loss. When treated quickly with successful uterine replacement and appropriate supportive care, most patients recover without long-term sequelae. However, delayed management may increase the need for surgical intervention, transfusion, and, in severe cases, can result in maternal mortality.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-30895.s12" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>Complications of uterine inversion may result from the condition itself or its management. Primary complications are driven by severe postpartum hemorrhage and hemodynamic instability, which can lead to shock, multiorgan injury, tissue necrosis, Sheehan syndrome, and the need for hysterectomy. Delayed recognition increases the risk of massive transfusion, infection, and surgical intervention. Secondary complications are related to treatments, including risks associated with anesthesia and blood transfusion.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-30895.s13" sec-type="Deterrence and Patient Education">
        <title>Deterrence and Patient Education</title>
        <p>Deterrence of uterine inversion focuses on appropriate management of the third stage of labor, including avoidance of excessive umbilical cord traction and fundal pressure before placental separation. Patient education should emphasize the importance of delivering in a setting with skilled obstetric care and seeking immediate attention for symptoms such as severe postpartum bleeding, pelvic pain, or a sensation of a vaginal mass.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-30895.s14" sec-type="Pearls and Other Issues">
        <title>Pearls and Other Issues</title>
        <p>Uterine inversion is a rare but potentially life-threatening obstetric emergency that requires rapid recognition and prompt uterine replacement. Key pearls include noting the absence of the fundus on abdominal exam and the presence of a vaginal mass, followed by manual or hydrostatic reduction, uterine relaxation, and subsequent uterotonics. Patients should receive urgent resuscitation, blood products if needed, and management in a high-acuity setting. Pitfalls include delayed diagnosis, excessive cord traction, and premature placental removal, all of which can worsen hemorrhage. Prevention focuses on careful management of the third stage of labor, avoiding fundal pressure before placental separation, and recognizing high-risk situations such as uterine atony or fundal placental implantation.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Intentional Uterine Inversion for Placental Removal</bold>
</p>
        <p>The TURIP (tourniquet, uterus inversion, and placental dissection) technique is used for managing placenta accreta spectrum without placenta previa during cesarean delivery. After exteriorizing the uterus and retained placenta, the fundus is elevated while the cervix and infundibulopelvic ligaments are temporarily occluded with a tourniquet to control blood flow. Nitroglycerin is administered to relax the uterus, allowing manual inversion to expose the placental attachment. The placenta is carefully dissected, and the uterine defect is repaired using knotless barbed sutures to minimize bleeding and reinforce the myometrium. After removing the tourniquet, the uterus is reverted to its normal position, and a hemostatic balloon can be placed to maintain intrauterine pressure and ensure hemostasis. This method allows direct visualization of the bleeding site, reduces the need for hysterectomy, and can be adapted for severe postpartum hemorrhage after vaginal delivery or incomplete placental separation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30895.r30">[30]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-30895.s15" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>Uterine inversion involves descent of the uterine fundus into the uterine cavity, through the cervix, or beyond the vaginal introitus. Most cases occur during the postpartum period and frequently arise during the third stage of labor. Fundal invagination disrupts normal uterine contraction and placental separation, leading to severe postpartum hemorrhage and potential hypovolemic shock. Contributing factors include excessive umbilical cord traction, fundal pressure, uterine atony, abnormal placentation, rapid labor, uterine overdistension, and a short umbilical cord. Patients commonly present with brisk vaginal bleeding, pelvic or abdominal pain, a palpable vaginal mass, and the absence of the uterine fundus on abdominal examination. Diagnosis relies primarily on clinical assessment, with ultrasound findings demonstrating characteristic uterine deformation. Immediate management focuses on hemodynamic stabilization, prompt manual reduction of the uterus, and escalation to pharmacologic or surgical interventions when reduction fails.</p>
        <p>Interprofessional collaboration improves patient safety and outcomes by ensuring rapid recognition and coordinated treatment of this obstetric emergency. Obstetricians and emergency physicians lead diagnosis and perform uterine reduction, while anesthesiologists support airway management, analgesia, and uterine relaxation when required. Primary care clinicians and advanced practitioners facilitate early recognition, risk assessment, and referral. Nurses coordinate emergency response, monitor hemodynamic status, administer fluids and medications, and provide continuous patient assessment. Pharmacists assist with the preparation and safe administration of uterotonics, tocolytics, and transfusion-related medications. Blood bank personnel ensure the rapid availability of blood products. Effective communication, shared decision-making, and structured team responses support timely intervention, prevent complications such as hemorrhagic shock, and promote comprehensive postpartum follow-up.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-30895.s16">
        <title>Review Questions</title>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>
